==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{m|ine-pro|*steup-}}. Cognates include {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|τύπτω||I strike|tr=týpto|lang=grc}}, {{etyl|sa|-}} {{term|sc=Deva|तोपति|tr=tópati||to hurt|lang=sa}}, {{etyl|sq|-}} {{term|shtyj||to thrust|lang=sq}}, {{etyl|cu|-}} {{term|тъпати|tr=tŭpati|lang=cu}}, and {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|styntan|lang=ang}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{term|stint|lang=en}}), {{term|stybb|lang=an}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{term|stub|lang=en}}), {{term|steap|lang=ang}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{term|steep|lang=en}})

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈstu.pe.oː/|lang=la}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|stupeō|stupēre|stupuī|stupītum|new=yes|conj=2|pattern=nopass}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=la}} I am [[stunned]], [[stiffened]] or [[benumbed]], [[stop]], [[hesitate]].
# {{context|intransitive|lang=la}} I am [[dazed]], [[speechless]] or [[silenced]]; I am [[astounded]], [[confounded]], [[aghast]] or [[amazed]].
#* '''c. 50 {{C.E.}}''', [[w:Seneca the Younger|Seneca the Younger]], ''[[w:Phaedra (Seneca)|Phaedra]], 607
#*: Curae leues locuntur, ingentes '''stupent'''.
#*:: Trivial concerns talk, great ones are '''speechless'''.
# {{context|transitive|lang=la}} I am [[astonished]] or [[amazed]] at, [[wonder]] at.

